# T-9.12 self-scan policy (task.md section 13 "self-scan clean").
# Loaded only when the scan TARGET ROOT is this repository; external
# targets never read it.  Two kinds of entries, both first-class in the
# accuracy layer and visible in the audit output:
#   * path rules (contain "/" or end with "/")  -> walk-time exclusion
#   * bare ids / bare filenames                 -> finding-level suppression
# Reasons live on their own "#" lines: a rule line is taken verbatim
# (the loader does not strip trailing comments).

# --- scope: non-product content, excluded at walk time -------------------
# generated caches, vendored corpora, downloaded artifacts
data/
# third-party downloads / scratch extracts (also holds stale copies of
# core/ rule-mapping files that would double-count)
dump/
# prose documentation, not source
docs/
# intentional vulnerability fixtures + seeded sample strings by design
tests/
# local scratch workspace (csv/txt/py ad-hoc files, not product code)
Working folder/
# single-file LLM01 eval fixture
integration_fixture_llm01/
# intentional attack-payload fixtures by design
payloads/
# historical metrics snapshots (generated report data, not source;
# distinct from baseline/sast_baseline.json which is read directly by
# the suppression loader, never via the walk)
baseline/

# --- rule-data / eval-fixture self-matches (finding-level) ---------------
# engine rule tables, CWE maps and governance mappings flag their own
# pattern/prose content when the engine scans its source (rule-data
# self-match: the detection patterns are literally present in these files)
llm_security.py
atlas_mappings.py
owasp_mappings.py
fix_patch_intelligence.py
llm_context_precision.py
# ground-truth / benchmark fixture tables: the vulnerable snippets ARE
# the corpus by design (eval content, not product defects)
benchmark_llm_precision_controlled.py
benchmark_sast_corpus.py
benchmark_evidence_validation_v1.py
